#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128969 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84167caa-9fa6-5162-a598-c2dd11d9ed85" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/id #uuid "fdf51620-58e9-5886-93e5-02a814b154bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8905" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=e^x$ that lies between the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=0$ to $x_2={2}$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{x}) = e^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}y\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{2}e^{x}\\sqrt{1 + (e^{x})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{2}e^{x}\\sqrt{1 + e^{2x}}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128970 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbff4737-2ea0-5d37-aa9e-395c4dc19b6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "ae72af2a-ec7e-569d-89a8-550c8eeabdbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-8909" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ that lies between the lines $x=2$ and $x=4$. Calculate the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $x_1=2$ to $x_2=4$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sqrt{x - 2}) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x - 2}}⇒(\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2} = (1)/(4(x - 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}y\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x - 2}\\sqrt{1 + (1)/(4(x - 2))}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x - 2}\\sqrt{(4x - 7)/(4(x - 2))}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{x - 2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{4x - 7}}{2\\sqrt{x - 2}})dx \\\\\n&= π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x - 7}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=4x-7 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\textrm du = 4\\,\\textrm dx\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= π∫_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{4x - 7}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4}∫_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{u^{3}} \\mid _{1}^{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}(27 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{13π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128971 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "fcfc9339-efd3-525f-a6f3-695124724e1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9120" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^3-y$ that lies in the second quadrant between its $y$-intercepts. What integral gives the surface area generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1994/Source/Images/2e7b78757d93bacc9371bde4d7088279.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} y \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{3} - y &= 0 \\\\\ny(y^{2} - 1) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}(y^{3} - y) = 3y^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{y_{1}}^{y_{2}}y\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dx}{dy})^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{1 + (3y^{2} - 1)^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}y\\sqrt{2 - 6y^{2} + 9y^{4}}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e70c003-febf-5ec6-bc57-224282eb5bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81f78a7c-cd88-56d1-8f8c-3b34e686486a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/4}\\tan y\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{2} y}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125889} {:answer/id #uuid "97af915f-c569-501c-b73b-a38405a2a1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/4}y\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{4} y}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125890} {:answer/id #uuid "caf0c590-9ab9-59be-9b17-711d20f78e60" :answer/type {:db/i